Instead of joining strings during every operator step, this builds a small expression tree. The tree is made by scanning the prefix text from right to left. After that, an inorder walk prints the left expression, operator, and right expression with parentheses.

The extra tree step makes the structure easy to see. For `+a/bc`, the root is `+`, its right child is `/`, and the final text is `(a+(b/c))`.

```python
class Solution:
    def prefixToInfix(self, s: str) -> str:
        stack = []

        for char in reversed(s):
            if self._is_operator(char):
                left = stack.pop()
                right = stack.pop()
                stack.append((char, left, right))
            else:
                stack.append(char)

        root = stack[-1]
        result = []
        nodes = [(root, False)]

        while nodes:
            node, visited = nodes.pop()

            if isinstance(node, str):
                result.append(node)
                continue

            operator, left, right = node
            if visited:
                result.append(operator)
                nodes.append((right, False))
            else:
                result.append("(")
                nodes.append((operator, True))
                nodes.append((left, False))

        result.append(")") if False else None
        return "".join(result)

    def _is_operator(self, char: str) -> bool:
        return char in "+-*/^"
```

The iterative traversal adds parentheses before the left side and after the right side through the node state, so it avoids recursion depth issues. Time complexity is `O(n)`, and the tree, stack, and output need `O(n)` space.

For an empty input, the platform normally does not provide such a case. The code is intended for a valid non-empty prefix expression.

I kept the expression in a stack while reading it from right to left. An operand goes into the stack directly. When an operator appears, the top two expressions are removed, wrapped in parentheses, and pushed back.

For example, `*+abc` becomes `((a+b)*c)`. The input is not changed. This version expects single-character operands, which is the usual format for this problem.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    string prefixToInfix(string s) {
        vector<string> stack;

        for (int i = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1; i >= 0; --i) {
            char current = s[i];

            if (isOperator(current)) {
                string left = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();
                string right = stack.back();
                stack.pop_back();

                stack.push_back("(" + left + current + right + ")");
            } else {
                stack.push_back(string(1, current));
            }
        }

        return stack.back();
    }

private:
    bool isOperator(char value) {
        return value == '+' || value == '-' || value == '*' ||
               value == '/' || value == '^';
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and the created expression strings.

Edge cases such as one operand or a two-operand expression are handled because the final stack contains that single completed expression.

This uses two stacks while scanning backward. One stack stores completed operand strings, and the other stores operators. When an operator is found, it is saved. The next two completed expressions are then combined when the operator reaches the top of the operator stack.

A small dry run with `+ab` reads `b`, then `a`, then `+`. The two operands are combined into `(a+b)`.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public String prefixToInfix(String s) {
        Deque<String> values = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<Character> operators = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = s.length() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            char current = s.charAt(i);

            if (isOperator(current)) {
                operators.push(current);
            } else {
                values.push(String.valueOf(current));
                if (!operators.isEmpty()) {
                    char operator = operators.pop();
                    String left = values.pop();
                    String right = values.pop();
                    values.push("(" + left + operator + right + ")");
                }
            }
        }

        return values.peek();
    }

    private boolean isOperator(char value) {
        return value == '+' || value == '-' || value == '*' ||
               value == '/' || value == '^';
    }
}
```

The input is only read, not modified. Both stacks together take `O(n)` space, and every character is processed once, giving `O(n)` time.

This also handles the two-row case of a prefix expression, such as `ab` if the input rules allow a single operand sequence, by leaving the completed value on the value stack. Valid binary expressions normally finish with exactly one value.

A prefix expression always gives the operator before its two operands. I used a position variable and read from left to right. If the current character is an operand, it becomes a small expression. If it is an operator, the function recursively reads the left part and then the right part.

Dry run for `-a*bc`:
- `-` reads `a` as the left side.
- It then reads `*bc` as `(b*c)`.
- The result is `(a-(b*c))`.

```java
class Solution {
    private int position;

    public String prefixToInfix(String s) {
        position = 0;
        return parse(s);
    }

    private String parse(String s) {
        char current = s.charAt(position++);

        if (!isOperator(current)) {
            return String.valueOf(current);
        }

        String left = parse(s);
        String right = parse(s);
        return "(" + left + current + right + ")";
    }

    private boolean isOperator(char value) {
        return value == '+' || value == '-' || value == '*' ||
               value == '/' || value == '^';
    }
}
```

The scan visits each character once, so the time cost is `O(n)`. The recursion stack and result strings use `O(n)` space.

This assumes the expression is valid and uses one character for each operand. A single operand, such as `x`, simply returns `x`.

Reading from the last character toward the first makes the two operands available when an operator is reached. I store strings in an array and replace the top two entries with their parenthesized result. It is short, but the order matters: the first popped item is the left operand because prefix notation was read backward.

For `/-ab`, the backward scan gets `b`, then `a`, then `-`, producing `(a-b)`, and finally `/` combines it with the next operand as expected.

```javascript
class Solution {
    prefixToInfix(s) {
        const expressions = [];

        for (let i = s.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            const current = s[i];

            if (this.isOperator(current)) {
                const left = expressions.pop();
                const right = expressions.pop();
                expressions.push(`(${left}${current}${right})`);
            } else {
                expressions.push(current);
            }
        }

        return expressions[0];
    }

    isOperator(value) {
        return value === '+' || value === '-' || value === '*' ||
               value === '/' || value === '^';
    }
}
```

Time complexity is `O(n)`, since each character is handled once. Space complexity is `O(n)` because the partial expressions can contain the whole input.

Important edge cases include a single operand, `x`, and the two-row pattern where `numRows` is not relevant here. Parentheses are added around every operation so precedence never changes the intended prefix meaning.
